# .gitignore — SDG App (com.ibtidaatech.sdgapp)
#
# Shipped in every zip from zip 20 onward. If you change it on your machine,
# note it in LOCAL_CHANGES.md or the next zip will overwrite your edit.

# ===========================================================================
# 1. RELEASE SIGNING — the one that cannot be undone
# ===========================================================================
# Two different disasters live here:
#
#   * Commit the keystore to a public repo and anyone can sign an app as
#     Ibtidaa Tech. Play Store will accept their build as an update to
#     yours. Removing the file later does not help, because it stays in git
#     history forever — which is exactly what happened with the Gemini key
#     in the EmpowHer repo.
#   * LOSE the keystore and you can never update this app again. Not
#     "it is difficult" — Play Store will refuse a differently-signed
#     update, and the only route left is publishing under a new package
#     name and asking every user to reinstall.
#
# So: never in git, and backed up somewhere that is not this folder.
# Keep a copy in two places you control, with the passwords recorded
# separately from the file.
*.jks
*.keystore
key.properties
android/key.properties
**/key.properties
upload-keystore.jks
release-keystore.jks

# ===========================================================================
# 2. SECRETS
# ===========================================================================
# The AI provider keys live on the Render proxy as environment variables and
# must never appear in this repo. The proxy reads them from the hosting
# dashboard, not from a file you commit.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*serviceAccount*.json
*service-account*.json
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json

# ===========================================================================
# 3. Firebase config — not secret, but still kept out
# ===========================================================================
# These files carry an `AIza...` Web API key that is a public identifier,
# not a credential: it ships inside every APK and is readable by anyone who
# unzips one. Your Firestore rules are what protect the data, which is also
# why the AI keys sit on the proxy instead.
#
# They are ignored for two other reasons:
#   * They are generated per machine by `flutterfire configure`, so a
#     committed copy goes stale and causes confusing build errors.
#   * On the Spark free tier, publishing your project identifiers makes it
#     cheap for someone to point traffic at your Firestore and exhaust the
#     daily free quota, which takes the app down until it resets. Firebase
#     App Check is the real fix; this just does not hand it over.
#
# If you would rather the repo clone-and-build on a new machine, delete
# these four lines and commit them — it is a defensible choice, not a
# security hole.
lib/firebase_options.dart
android/app/google-services.json
ios/Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist
macos/Runner/GoogleService-Info.plist
.firebase/
firebase-debug.log
firestore-debug.log
ui-debug.log

# ===========================================================================
# 4. Flutter / Dart build output
# ===========================================================================
.dart_tool/
.packages
.pub-cache/
.pub/
build/
.flutter-plugins
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
*.dart.js
*.info.json
*.js.deps
*.js.map
coverage/
doc/api/

# Release artefacts. Keep built APKs and bundles out of git — they are
# large, they change every build, and git stores every version forever.
*.apk
*.aab
*.apks
*.ipa
*.app.zip

# pubspec.lock IS committed on purpose. This is an application, not a
# package, and the lock file is what makes a build reproducible — it is how
# the `intl` version stays pinned to what your SDK accepts.
#
# .metadata is also committed on purpose: `flutter upgrade` reads it to
# migrate the platform folders.

# ===========================================================================
# 5. Android
# ===========================================================================
android/.gradle/
android/.idea/
android/captures/
android/local.properties
android/app/debug/
android/app/profile/
android/app/release/
android/gradle-wrapper.jar
*.hprof

# gradle.properties is committed, because it carries the
# `kotlin.incremental=false` line that this project needs on Windows when
# the project and the pub cache are on different drives. See LOCAL_CHANGES.md.

# ===========================================================================
